  "page1": {
    "title": "What Fire Really Needs",
    "subtitle": "Fire needs three things to burn, and taking one away puts it out.",
    "body": "Strike a match, and a small flame jumps up. But fire is not magic. Burning needs three things at the same time. It needs fuel, like wood or paper, to feed the flame. It needs oxygen, a gas that fills the air around us. It also needs heat to start and to keep going. We call these three the fire triangle. When all three meet, fire burns. If you take even one away, the fire dies. Water cools the fuel below its heat point. A blanket blocks the oxygen, so the flame cannot breathe. <mark>Putting out</mark> a fire means breaking the triangle. That is why firefighters aim at one weak side.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/venus/2026-07/11.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A fire triangle of fuel, oxygen, and heat, and how water or a blanket breaks it to stop a flame.",
